<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Auxilia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as auxiliary troops</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The auxilia were the non-citizen troops of the Roman imperial army, recruited from provincial peoples. They provided infantry, cavalry and specialist units such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a>, serving alongside the citizen legions. After long service they received Roman citizenship, which helped integrate provincials into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/auxilia.jpg" alt="Auxilia" width="320" height="221" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CristianChirita · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Engineering_corps_traian_s_column_river_crossing.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Republican legion was heavy infantry with few horsemen: around 200 BCE a legion of 4,200 infantry had only 300 cavalry and no slingers or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a>. Until then most of a Roman army's cavalry came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s Italian allies, organised in alae, until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/social-war-91-87-bce">Social War</a> of 91 to 88 BCE.</p><p>Some experienced indigenous units were kept after the civil wars ended in 31 BCE, and from 27 BCE Augustus created the regular auxilia as a corps of non-citizen troops parallel to the legions. Unlike the part-time <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conscription">conscripts</a> of the Republican allies, who served in units raised and disbanded for campaigns, the Augustan auxilia were mainly volunteer professionals in permanent units.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Auxiliaries were recruited chiefly from the peregrini, free provincial subjects without Roman citizenship, and could even be recruited from outside Roman territory. In the Julio-Claudian period, 27 BCE to 68 CE, they changed from motley levies into a standing corps with standard equipment and conditions, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> appears to have developed them considerably. Regiments stationed near home carried a risk. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arminius">Arminius</a> served as prefect of an auxiliary unit before leading German tribes to destroy three legions in the Teutoberg Forest in 9 CE.</p><p>The Batavi supplied one ala and eight <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cohort">cohorts</a>, about 5,000 men, though they were a tiny share of the empire's population, and their revolt of 69 to 70 led the Flavian emperors to station regiments away from their home provinces as standard policy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>By the 2nd century the auxilia held as many infantry as the legions and almost all the cavalry, light cavalry and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a>, three fifths of the regular land forces, and outnumbered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">legionaries</a> by 1.5 to 1. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dacia">Dacia</a> together held over a quarter of the regiments. An auxiliary is generally thought to have been paid a third of a legionary's wage, 300 sesterces a year.</p><p>Regiments were named by type, number and the people they were raised from, as in cohors III Batavorum, and could win honorific titles. The most sought after was c.R. (civium Romanorum), which gave citizenship to the members then serving. From <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a>, only Roman knights could command a regiment. In the 4th century the army was restructured, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/notitia-dignitatum">Notitia Dignitatum</a> lists about 70 alae and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cohort">cohortes</a> that kept their 2nd-century names.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Auxilia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At its top stood a mounted warrior elite of steppe origin; beneath it lived Gepids, Romanised provincials, Kutrigurs, Bulgars and, above all, Slavs, who formed much of the population and provided farmers and <b>auxiliary troops</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/avar-khaganate">Avar Khaganate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The treaties of Tiberius Sempronius Gracchus in 179 BCE fixed obligations for many towns, including tribute, <b>auxiliary troops</b> and a ban on founding new fortified towns.</blockquote><a href="/empire/celtiberians">Celtiberians · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Infantry, <b>auxiliary troops</b> and Bedouin contingents supported the cavalry, while fleets operated in the Mediterranean and Red Sea.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Roman legion, a professional heavy infantry unit of roughly 5,000 men supported by a comparable number of <b>auxiliary troops</b>, was the instrument of Roman expansion and the basis of imperial security.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-empire">Roman Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Chapters 12 to 14 turn to arms; he stands strongly against mercenaries and <b>auxiliary troops</b>, and holds that a prince's main concern should be war, or the preparation for it.</blockquote><a href="/work/the-prince">The Prince · What It Is</a></li><li><blockquote>Its capital was Caesarea (Cherchell), the former royal city of Juba II, and it was governed by an equestrian procurator with <b>auxiliary troops</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/mauretania-caesariensis">Mauretania Caesariensis</a></li></ul></section>
